# templates/drivers/grok.driver — the xAI Grok Build CLI driver:
#   multiplexer = herdr · agent runtime = xAI Grok Build CLI (`grok`).
#
# A non-Claude agent-runtime driver (HERD-178, agent-runtime portability epic HERD-150). It swaps
# ONLY the RUNTIME — the `claude`-specific exec surface (spawn / one-shot / resume / model-switch /
# permission-mode / limit / session / cost) is rebound to Grok Build's real control surface — while
# KEEPING herdr as the multiplexer, exactly as herdr-claude.driver's header notes ("a non-Claude
# runtime rebinds this block, keeping herdr as the mux"). Model this file on
# templates/drivers/herdr-claude.driver (default) + headless.driver. See docs/driver-abstraction.md.
#
# CONTRACT (mirrors .herd/config, same as every driver): shell-sourceable KEY=value, COMMITTED,
# ZERO-SECRET — command SHAPES only, never credentials or absolute host paths ({{SCRIPTS_DIR}} is
# resolved at render time). Selected per project by `.herd/config` key HERD_DRIVER=grok.
#
# RENDER SCOPE: templates/coordinator.md.tmpl tokenizes only the four MUX capabilities
# ({{DRIVER_LIST_AGENTS}} {{DRIVER_FOCUS_AGENT}} {{DRIVER_SEND_TEXT}} {{DRIVER_SWITCH_MODEL}}), which
# this driver binds to the SAME herdr surface as the default — so `HERD_DRIVER=grok herd render`
# resolves the binding table with NO missing-binding crash and NO leftover {{token}}. The
# DRIVER_AGENT_* exec block below is data the P2–P4 routing (Brian's queue) consumes; nothing renders
# it yet. Proof: tests/test-grok-driver.sh.
#
# FLAGS ARE RESEARCHED, NOT GUESSED. Every grok flag below is from the canonical Grok Build CLI docs:
# docs.x.ai/build/cli/headless-scripting, docs.x.ai/build/cli/reference and (for the rules-injection
# finding below) docs.x.ai/build/features/project-rules — interactive `grok`; `-p,--single <PROMPT>`
# ("send one prompt"); `-m,--model <MODEL>`; `--output-format {plain|json|streaming-json}`;
# `--always-approve` ("auto-approve tool executions", alias `--yolo`); session flags
# `-s,--session-id <UUID>`, `-r,--resume [<ID>]`, `-c,--continue` ("continue the most recent session
# for the current directory" — a genuinely documented session-continuation mechanism, not an
# assumption); `--rules <TEXT>` ("extra rules appended to the system prompt"); and the `/model` TUI
# command. RE-AUDITED 2026-08-18 (HERD-767, epic HERD-754 step 8) against those three pages: this pass
# found and fixed one real drift (see the CONTEXT-INJECTION note on DRIVER_AGENT_INTERACTIVE_SPAWN
# below) and tightened DRIVER_AGENT_RESUME to the one prompt-delivery shape every fetched doc example
# actually demonstrates. The grok binary itself remains NOT INSTALLED on this dev machine (`command -v
# grok` fails here) — every claim below is a documentation claim, not a live-verified one; see each
# binding's comment for exactly what is confirmed by docs vs. still only an assumption.

# ── MUX capabilities (herdr) — IDENTICAL to herdr-claude: this driver swaps the RUNTIME, not the
#    multiplexer, so every herdr incantation below is byte-for-byte the default's. ──────────────────
DRIVER_LIST_AGENTS='herdr agent list'
DRIVER_FOCUS_AGENT='herdr agent focus <slug>'
DRIVER_SEND_TEXT='herdr pane run <agent-pane> "<text>"; herdr pane send-keys <agent-pane> Enter'
DRIVER_SWITCH_MODEL='herdr pane run <builder-agent-pane> "/model <MODEL_FEATURE value>"'

# ── MUX capabilities the LANE SCRIPTS + watcher exercise (bound so the driver covers the FULL
#    mux surface, not just the four the skill names inline). Same herdr surface as the default. ─────
DRIVER_START_AGENT='bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/herd-feature.sh <slug> "<task>"'
DRIVER_CREATE_TAB='herdr tab create --workspace <ws>'
DRIVER_READ_PANE='herdr pane read <pane>'
DRIVER_SEND_KEYS='herdr pane send-keys <pane> <keys>'

# ── Agent-runtime EXEC bindings (HERD-150 P1 capability table, rebound for Grok Build) ────────────
# Each key is Grok Build's real incantation for its capability class — the grok counterpart of the
# exact `claude …` strings herdr-claude.driver carries. Same contract: command SHAPES, zero-secret.
#
# ABSENT-BINDING DEGRADATION CONTRACT. A capability the runtime does NOT expose as a stable,
# verifiable contract (Grok's usage-limit banner text; the exact session token-usage JSON schema + a
# grok-model→USD price table) is bound to an explicit `@degrade:<reason>` sentinel rather than a
# GUESSED value. The sentinel is deliberately fail-safe on every consumer: as a regex it can never
# match real runtime output (so limit-detection simply never false-fires and falls through to the
# runtime-independent hook-sentinel + resume backstop — see docs/driver-abstraction.md § limit-
# detection); as a space-separated key list it names no real JSON field (so a usage sum finds nothing
# and cost.sh surfaces it as `unpriced`/zero, its documented graceful degrade). It is always non-empty
# so no consumer crashes under `set -u`, and self-documents WHY the capability degraded. This is the
# honest alternative to inventing a rate-limit regex or a token schema the runtime never promised.

# interactive-spawn — open a Grok TUI seeded with the task prompt; --always-approve = hands-off tools.
# CONTEXT-INJECTION (grok-context-injection): CORRECTED 2026-08-18 (HERD-767, epic HERD-754 step 8).
# The prior comment here claimed "Grok does NOT auto-load CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md the way Claude Code
# does" — that premise was WRONG, and re-verifying it caught a second, more serious bug alongside it.
# docs.x.ai/build/features/project-rules is explicit that Grok auto-discovers project rules on its own:
# "Grok loads rules in this order, with deeper files taking precedence on conflicts: 1. Global rules
# in ~/.grok/ 2. Every directory from the repo root down to the working directory" — it names AGENTS.md
# and CLAUDE.md by name among the files it searches for. So a grok builder spawned with cwd inside its
# worktree already gets AGENTS.md for free, with NO flag required. Separately, the flag this binding
# used to inject it with — `--append-rules-to-system-prompt` — does not exist in either canonical flags
# page (docs.x.ai/build/cli/reference documents `--rules <TEXT>` — "extra rules appended to the system
# prompt" — as the real single-run rules-injection flag; a Claude-Code-flag-name alias the same
# reference page lists, `--append-system-prompt`, is also NOT `--append-rules-to-system-prompt`). A
# headless spawn built from the old value would have failed outright on a real grok binary with an
# unrecognized-argument error — this is exactly the kind of guessed-flag drift this audit exists to
# catch. Fixed to the real flag; the token stays bound (harmless, additive, and belt-and-suspenders
# against a builder cwd shallower than the repo root) rather than removed, since `--rules` costs
# nothing when Grok's own auto-load already covers the common case. FAIL-SOFT is unchanged: when there
# is no AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md the composer DROPS the flag+value pair (scripts/herd/driver.sh), so the
# spawn is byte-identical to the plain `grok --model <model> --always-approve "<prompt>"` shape.
# Claude/headless bindings carry no <agents-rules> token, so their argv is unaffected either way.
DRIVER_AGENT_INTERACTIVE_SPAWN='grok --model <model> --always-approve --rules <agents-rules> "<prompt>"'
# one-shot-exec — headless, prints and exits. `-p,--single` is Grok Build's `claude -p` analog. Every
# headless example on docs.x.ai/build/cli/headless-scripting and .../reference sends its prompt via
# `-p`, never as a bare positional argument, so this shape is the best-grounded one documented.
# STRUCTURED-OUTPUT FINDING (hunted for per HERD-767's audit): grok DOES have a `codex exec --json`
# analog — `--output-format {plain|json|streaming-json}` is real and documented ("one JSON object at
# the end" / "newline-delimited JSON events"). EXISTENCE is confirmed; the actual event/object SCHEMA
# is not — no example payload is shown on either docs page, and the binary is not installed here to
# observe one. Building a grok-exec-adapter (the analog of scripts/herd/codex-exec-adapter.sh) on a
# guessed schema would be exactly the fabrication this driver avoids everywhere else, so this binding
# stays plain-text (no --output-format bound) rather than guess at the JSON shape; that adapter is
# left for a future step once a machine with `grok` installed can observe a real stream.
DRIVER_AGENT_ONESHOT_EXEC='grok -p "<prompt>" --model <model> --always-approve'
# resume — continue the most recent session in the cwd with full context. `-c,--continue` ("continue
# the most recent session for the current directory") is confirmed genuinely documented, not an
# assumption — Grok analog of `claude --continue` (`-r,--resume [<ID>]` / `-s,--session-id <UUID>`
# target a specific session instead). TIGHTENED 2026-08-18: the prior binding sent the resumed prompt
# as a bare trailing positional (`grok --continue --always-approve "<prompt>"`); no fetched doc example
# ever sends a headless prompt that way — every single example (plain, --model, --output-format
# variants) uses `-p`. Switched to `-p "<prompt>" --continue --always-approve` so prompt delivery uses
# the one shape docs actually demonstrate; combining `-p` with `-c` in the same invocation is still not
# shown in an example, so that specific combination remains a documentation-grounded composition of two
# independently-confirmed flags, not a live-verified one — flag the PR calls this out explicitly.
DRIVER_AGENT_RESUME='grok -p "<prompt>" --continue --always-approve'
# model-switch — the `/model <model>` TUI slash command, delivered via the mux DRIVER_SEND_TEXT.
# CONFIRMED (not just assumed by Claude-Code analogy): docs.x.ai/build/overview lists it verbatim among
# Grok Build's own top-level CLI usage patterns ("`/model <name>` — Switch models within the TUI").
DRIVER_AGENT_MODEL_SWITCH='/model <model>'
# permission-mode flag — Grok's auto-approve-tool-executions flag (alias `--yolo`, confirmed on
# docs.x.ai/build/cli/reference), the analog of Claude's `--dangerously-skip-permissions`: a hands-off
# builder in an isolated worktree. Overridable via HERD_CLAUDE_FLAGS.
DRIVER_AGENT_PERMISSION_FLAG='--always-approve'
# limit-detection — DEGRADED: RE-CHECKED 2026-08-18 against docs.x.ai/build/cli/reference and
# .../headless-scripting — neither documents a usage-limit banner string, and no doc surfaces one
# (`/usage` shows a credit balance in the TUI, not a scrapeable banner). Still no verifiable stable
# contract, so this stays @degrade rather than guessing a regex. Fail-safe: never matches → falls
# through to the hook sentinel.
DRIVER_AGENT_LIMIT_PATTERN='@degrade:no-verified-grok-usage-limit-banner'
# session-identity — Grok Build stores headless sessions under ~/.grok/sessions (keyed by
# --session-id), rebindable via $HERD_TRANSCRIPT_ROOT. Real Grok equivalent of ~/.claude/projects.
# CONFIRMED verbatim on docs.x.ai/build/cli/headless-scripting: "Headless sessions are stored in
# ~/.grok/sessions".
DRIVER_AGENT_SESSION_ID='${HERD_TRANSCRIPT_ROOT:-$HOME/.grok/sessions}/<session-id>'
# cost/telemetry parse — DEGRADED: RE-CHECKED 2026-08-18 — Grok's session token-usage JSON keys and a
# grok-model→USD price table are still not documented anywhere on docs.x.ai (the headless-scripting and
# reference pages describe flags and output formats, never a usage/cost object shape), so this remains
# not a verifiable stable contract here. Fail-safe: names no real field → cost.sh sums nothing and marks
# the model `unpriced` rather than reporting a fabricated $0.
DRIVER_AGENT_COST_USAGE_KEYS='@degrade:no-verified-grok-usage-token-schema'
# process-signature (HERD-428) — the pane-cmdline substring(s) that identify a LIVE Grok agent in a
# pane's foreground process list; the control-room pane-role probe (layout-reconcile.sh
# _reload_pane_role) reads this instead of a hardcoded 'claude' literal, so a grok pane classifies as
# 'agent' rather than falling through to 'busy'. Space-separated substrings, matched by plain `in`
# (never a regex) — see driver.sh's herd_driver_agent_process_signature.
DRIVER_AGENT_PROCESS_SIGNATURE='grok'

# ── Specialist-agent ROSTER bindings (HERD-667, epic HERD-666) ────────────────────────────────────
# VERIFIED-BY-REPORT, not on this machine. The grok binary is NOT installed here (HERD-666 pre-audit),
# so these two bindings come from the operator's hand-prototype on grok 1.0.3 rather than a local
# probe, and post-merge live verification is owned by the second operator. What was reported, and why
# each binding is shaped this way:
#   • ~/.grok/agents/ (USER scope) is honored; a repo-local ./.grok/agents/ is IGNORED. That is trap
#     T1 of the epic and the reason DEFINITION_MODE is `install`: the committed source in
#     .herd/agents/ must be PUBLISHED to the user-scope dir (`herd agents install`) or the runtime
#     never sees it. $HERD_AGENT_DEFINITION_ROOT rebinds the root (the hermetic-test / non-standard
#     -home knob), exactly as $HERD_TRANSCRIPT_ROOT rebinds DRIVER_AGENT_SESSION_ID above.
#   • `--agent <name>` selects a definition by name, and an UNKNOWN name is SILENTLY IGNORED — the
#     runtime just runs as a plain agent. That is trap T2, and it is why `herd agents verify` is
#     empirical and carries a negative control: on this runtime a probe without one cannot fail.
# Anything NOT reported stays @degrade rather than guessed (the same honesty rule the limit-pattern
# and cost-schema bindings above follow). `herd agents verify` fail-softs to a clear
# "grok binary not present — unverified on this machine" note, never a red row.
DRIVER_AGENT_DEFINITION_DIR='${HERD_AGENT_DEFINITION_ROOT:-$HOME}/.grok/agents'
DRIVER_AGENT_SELECT_FLAG='--agent <name>'
DRIVER_AGENT_DEFINITION_MODE='install'
